Consider electron–positron annihilation at center-of-mass energy near √s ≈ 10.6 GeV producing double-charmonium final states. Experimental measurements report:

(a) an inclusive cross section σ(e⁺e⁻ → J/ψ + cc̄ + X) substantially larger than predictions based on leading-order non-relativistic QCD (NRQCD) factorization;

(b) an exclusive cross section σ(e⁺e⁻ → J/ψ + η_c) (times branching fractions into ≥2 charged tracks) that exceeds leading-order NRQCD/perturbative QCD predictions by nearly an order of magnitude;

(c) an upper bound on σ(e⁺e⁻ → J/ψ + J/ψ) that is compatible with the corresponding theoretical predictions.

Within a consistent QCD-based framework (perturbative QCD plus NRQCD factorization, or any well-defined extension thereof such as resummation, relativistic corrections, light-cone distribution amplitudes, color-octet contributions, higher-order αs corrections, or a new short-distance production mechanism), identify and justify the theoretical ingredient(s) responsible for the gap between the leading-order predictions and the measured cross sections of channels (a) and (b), while remaining consistent with (c). In particular, determine whether higher-order perturbative corrections combined with appropriate scale-setting and relativistic corrections are sufficient to bring theory into agreement with experiment, or whether a genuinely new production mechanism beyond standard NRQCD factorization is required.

